# Chapter 2, part 1: templates, backups, revisions.
.h2 Templates [file-templates]
.ix Template
.ix Templates, why
.ix DEFAULT.SET
.ix Boilerplate text
.ix Standard form
A template is a document that stands for a kind of document.  It carries the
global format settings, the page layouts, the typefaces and the marker
settings that every document of that kind should have, and it can carry
standard text: the headings of a memo (Date, From, To, Subject), the numbered
sections of a contract, the title page of a report.  The New command names a
template, and the new document begins as a copy of it.  The template itself
is not changed.
.pic pictures/file-new.png 5 The New File panel, with the Template field.
.h3 Making a template file [file-templates-create]
.ix Template, creating
.ix Save Global Settings Only
.ix Template with text
There are two ways to make a template.
.table 4 57
Step | What to do
1 | Set up a document the way you want all documents of a kind to be: the
 | formats of the Global menu, the page layouts, the typefaces and the
 | marker settings.  Choose File, Save, move to Options and choose Save
 | Global Settings Only.  Type a name for the Document Name (without an
 | extension, the program adds {c:.set}).  The file that is written has all
 | the settings and no text.
2 | If the template should also give a starting text, write that text in the
 | document, choose File, Save with the option Save Entire Document, and
 | give the name an extension of {c:.set}, for example {c:memo.set}.  The file
 | is a complete document, and the Editor does not mind its name.
.end
.caution The Document Name field of the Save panel begins with the name of the
  document.  If you choose Save Global Settings Only and do not change the
  name, the name has an extension ({c:.doc}) and is used as it is: the document
  file is then replaced by a file that has the settings and no text.  The
  question "Overwrite old version on disk?" is asked, and its first answer is
  Yes.  Type a new name before you choose that option.
A template can be improved like any document: retrieve it, change it and save
it again.  A document that already exists can be given the settings of a
template with Retrieve Global Settings or Retrieve Typeface Settings Only
({n:file-retrieve}), without losing its text.
.h3 Starting a document from a template [file-templates-use]
.ix Template, using
.ix New File, Template
Choose File, New.  Type the name of the new document in Document Name, and in
the field Template the name of the template, with its extension, for example
{c:memo.set}; {k:F2} on that field lists the templates of the working
directory.  When there are templates in the directories of the list below, New
first shows that list ({n:file-new-list}) and puts the one that you choose in
the field Template.  The new document has the settings and the text of the
template.
The fields Description and Author are put in the document when they are not
empty, and blank ones keep those of the template.  {fig:file-new-description}
shows the panel after a template was chosen from the list and a description
was typed.  The field Revision is the
revision of the new document, 1.000 unless you type another.  The template
field is not given an extension: {c:memo} is not found if the file is
{c:memo.set}.
.pic pictures/file-new-description.png 5 The New File panel, with the template of the list and a description typed. [file-new-description]
.h3 Where the templates are looked for [file-templates-path]
.ix Template, search path
.ix STRATA_TEMPLATES
.ix Template directory
The name in the Template field is looked up in this order, and the first
place that has a file of that name, without regard to the case of the
letters, is used.
.table 5 55
Step | Place
1 | If the name contains a slash and is a path of the file system, exactly
 | that path (relative to the directory in which the Editor was started, not
 | to the Working Directory).
2 | The Working Directory of the panel.
3 | The directory named by the environment variable {c:STRATA_TEMPLATES}, if
 | it is set, and the subdirectories in it.
4 | The directory {c:templates} of the settings, and the subdirectories in it.
5 | The directory {c:share/strata/templates} beside the programs, and the
 | subdirectories in it.
6 | The directory in which the program file of the Editor is.
.end
The directories of the steps 3 to 5 may hold subdirectories, down to four
levels.  A name without a slash is looked for in the directory and in the
subdirectories below it, so the name {c:label-us-5160} finds the file in
{c:labels/avery/us}; the directory itself is looked in first, then the
subdirectories in the order of their names.  A name with a slash that is not a
path of the file system is a path below the directory, with the subdirectories
separated by a slash, such as {c:labels/avery/us/label-us-5160}.  The first file that is found
is used, so a template of a person's own hides a shipped template of the same
name.
The list that New shows ({n:file-new-list}) is made from three directories and
the subdirectories in them: the one named by {c:STRATA_TEMPLATES}, the
directory {c:templates} in the directory of the settings
({c:~/.strata/templates}), and the directory {c:share/strata/templates} of the
installation.  Subdirectories of the same name in the three directories are one
subdirectory in the list.  The place that the settings are kept in, {c:~/.strata},
is not searched for the name typed in the field.  See
{x:appcfg-search-templates} for the rule in the chapter on the configuration.
If no file is found and the name is {c:DEFAULT.SET}, which is the name that
the field has when the panel opens, the built-in settings are used: a box
titled "New document type" offers the entries Structured and Unstructured, and
an empty document of the kind that you choose is made.  Any
other name that is not found gives the message "Cannot find the template
file".  A file that is found but is not a document gives its reading error
with the title "Template".  So a person who wants every new document to have
the same settings puts a file called {c:DEFAULT.SET} in the working
directory, or in the directory of {c:STRATA_TEMPLATES}, and does not have to
type the name.
.h2 Backup files [file-backup]
.ix Backup file
.ix Backups Kept
.ix .BAK
.ix Backup, numbered
.ix Previous version of a file
.ix Global, Options, backup
A backup keeps a previous version of a document.  Save keeps up to 99 backups;
the setting Backups Kept (0 to 99, three by default) gives the number.  The
backups are numbered files in a directory named {c:.backup} in the directory
of the document, so that they do not fill the directory of the documents.  A
backup holds the document as it was before a Save.  Backup 1 is always the
newest and the highest number the oldest.
.table 14 46
Document | Backups
{c:report.doc} | {c:.backup/report.bak01}, {c:.backup/report.bak02} and so on
{c:REPORT.DOC} | {c:.backup/REPORT.BAK01}, {c:.backup/REPORT.BAK02} and so on
{c:my.report.doc} | {c:.backup/my.report.bak01} and so on
{c:memo.txt} | {c:.backup/memo.txt.bak01} and so on
.end
The number has two digits, so that the backups stay in order in a list of
files.
.h3 The directory .backup [file-backup-folder]
.ix Backup, directory .backup
.ix .backup, the directory
The directory is made when the first backup of a document is made, in the directory
of the document and with the mode that a new directory gets (the
{c:umask} of the account determines).  A document that has not changed makes no
backup and no directory, and with Backups Kept at 0 no directory is made.  The
checklists of files do not show the directory; to look into it, type its name in a
Document Name field.  If the directory cannot be made or written (the directory is
read-only, or a file called {c:.backup} is in the way), the document is still
saved, no backup is made, and the message line shows why.  If the directory is on
another disk than the document (it is a mount point), the old document is
copied into it instead of being linked.
.note Earlier versions of Strata left the backups beside the document, as
  {c:report.bak01}.  The programs do not move or remove these files, and the
  first Save in the new way does not ask about them.  The panel Restore Backup
  lists them, marked {c:old}, and restores them.  They can be moved into the
  directory {c:.backup} or removed by hand.
.h3 Choosing how many backups are kept [file-backup-option]
.ix Backups Kept
.ix Backup, how many
The number is the field {b:Backups Kept (0 to 99)} of the panel Options of the
Global menu in the Editor, and the field {b:Backups Kept (0 to 99)} of Setup in
the Document Manager.  It is a setting of the user and not of the document: it
is kept in the file {c:~/.strata/config} as {c:backups=3}, for instance, and
read when the Editor starts.  With the value 0 no backup is made.  The value 1
keeps the version before the last save.  Without a settings file the value is
{b:3}.  The number does not apply to the special backup of an older file
({n:file-backup-special}), which stays.

When the number is lowered (from 5 to 2, say) and the panel is accepted, a box
is shown first.  The box shows which backups are removed ({c:Backups Kept: 5 to
2.
The backups numbered 3, 4 and 5 are removed, in every folder .backup, the
next time each document is saved with a backup or a backup is restored; no other
time.}) and that {k:Y} keeps the change and {k:N} or {k:Esc} keeps the old
number.  The programs cannot visit every directory, so the extra backups of a
document go at its next save.  With {k:N} the rest of Setup is saved all the
same.  Raising the number, or leaving it, puts no question.  With the value 0 the
box shows that no more backups are made and that the backups that exist are
not touched.
.caution Every backup is a whole copy of the document.  A document of 2
  megabytes with 99 backups takes about 200 megabytes of disk.  Choose the
  lowest number that the work allows.
.h3 When a backup is made [file-backup-when]
.ix Backup, when made
.ix Backup, name of
.ix Backup, Ctrl-S
.ix Backup, Autosave
.ix Backup, unchanged document
The backups are made by Save Entire Document and by {k:Ctrl-S}, just before the
new version replaces the file, under these rules.
.table 29 31
Condition | Rule
Backups Kept is 0 | No backup; the backups that exist stay as they are.
There is no file of that name on disk | No backup (there is nothing to keep).
The document has not changed: the file on | Nothing is written and no backup is
 disk holds exactly what Save would write | made or moved.  The message line shows
 | so.
Save Global Settings Only, Extract | No backup (only Save Entire Document
 Current Section | makes them).
Autosave | No backup; see {x:file-backup-autosave}.
The backups are 1 to the number kept | Backup 1 is moved to 2, 2 to 3 and so on;
 | the oldest is removed; the version on
 | disk becomes backup 1.
A number is missing in the series | The backups below the gap move up into
 | it; no backup is removed.
The directory .backup cannot be made | The document is saved, no backup is made,
 or written | and the message line shows why.
Backups Kept was lowered | The backups above the new number are
 | removed at the next save that makes a
 | backup, or at a restore.
.end
The backup is a copy of the file that is on the disk, not of the text in the
Editor.  The new version is written completely before any backup is moved, and
only then does it replace the document, so that a complete document is on the
disk at every moment.  If a step fails (the directory is not writable, the disk
is full, a backup cannot be moved) a message reports this, the changes stay in
the Editor, and the document and its backups are as they were.  In a directory
that
is not writable, a document that may be changed is saved in its place and
no backup is made.  The question "Overwrite old version on disk?" of the Save
panel is about the document file; the backups are moved without a question.
.note The backup is the version before the last Save, not the version at the
  start of the editing session.  With Backups Kept at 3, the last three saved
  versions are the backups.  Autosave replaces the document on the disk but
  makes no backup, so after an Autosave backup 1 can be the version of the
  last Autosave.  To keep a version, save it under another name, as in the
  example in {x:file-examples}.
.h3 Using a backup [file-backup-use]
.ix Backup, restoring
.ix Retrieving a backup
.ix Restoring a document
To go back to a backup, use the entry {b:Restore Backup} of the Document
Manager.  Choose the document; the panel lists its backups, newest first, with
the number, the date and time, the size and the first words of each (the old
ones beside the document are marked {c:old}).  Choose a backup: {b:View the
text} shows its text in a pager that you leave with {k:Esc}, and {b:Restore
this backup} prompts, in a box, for {k:Y} or {k:N} ({k:Esc} is {k:N}).  A
restore
is a swap, not an overwrite: the present document becomes backup 1, the backups
below the one that was restored move up by one, and the chosen text becomes the
document, with its mode and owner as far as they can be kept.  With one backup
kept the document and the backup change names.  A backup that holds the same
text as the document changes nothing, and the panel reports this.  A restore
keeps
at least one backup whatever Backups Kept says, and removes the backups above
that number.  If the document is open in an Editor, that Editor keeps the old
text until the document is loaded again.  The steps are in an order in which a
failure loses no text: if one fails after the document was replaced, the old
document is the file {c:.backup/.swapNNN} that the message names.

File, Retrieve with the name of the backup typed in full,
{c:.backup/report.bak01}, also reads it; the backup then becomes the current
document with that name.  A Save onto a backup is refused, so a backup cannot be damaged by
mistake: {k:Ctrl-S} and File, Save with the name of a backup write nothing, and
the document stays changed.  A name is the name of a backup when its extension
is {c:.bak} or {c:.bak} and digits, such as {c:report.bak01}, in capitals or in
lower case, or when the file is in a directory that is named {c:.backup}.  To keep
your changes, use File, Save with another name.
.ix Backup, refused Save
.h3 The special backup [file-backup-special]
.ix Backup, special
.ix Special backup
.ix Backup, older version of a file
.ix File versions, special backup
When a file of an older version ({n:file-versions}) is loaded into software
that writes a newer version, the Editor saves a special backup of it when the
load is finished.  The special backup preserves the file as it was on the
disk, so that the older software can still read it.  It is saved next
to the file, not in the directory {c:.backup}, and its name has the form
{c:V<old-version>-<filename>.<extension>}: the letter V, the version of the
file, a hyphen, and the name of the file with its extension.
.table 22 38
File | Special backup
{c:memo.doc} | {c:V2-memo.doc}, when the file is version 2
{c:standard.set} | {c:V2-standard.set}, when the file is version 2
.end
If the special backup already exists, it is not written again, so the first
one stays as it was.  If it cannot be written (the directory cannot be
written to, or the disk is full), the load is refused, a message shows why,
and the file is not touched.
The special backup is permanent.  The setting Backups Kept does not count it
and does not remove it, the numbered backups ({n:file-backup}) never move it,
and it can be removed only by hand.  The panel Restore Backup lists it with
the backups of the document.  Restoring it writes the file back as it was, with
whatever version number it carries: a special backup of version 2
restores a document of version 2.
.h3 Autosave and the files [file-backup-autosave]
.ix Autosave
.ix Autosave Interval
.ix Autosave, revision
Autosave is a setting of the Document Manager (Setup: Autosave, Yes or No, and
Autosave Interval (Seconds), 1 to 86400; Yes and 120 by default) kept in
{c:~/.strata/config}.  The Editor does not show it on its Options panel.  When
Autosave is Yes and the document has unsaved changes, the Editor saves it under
its name when the interval has passed since the first unsaved change, without a
question and without a panel.  It does what {k:Ctrl-S} does, but it makes no
backup, so that the backups of the versions that you saved are not pushed out
by versions that you did not ask to save.  It raises the revision by one (see
{n:file-revision}).  A document that has no name is not saved.
.if .STRATA_BUILD_TYPE # release
For developers: the options {c:--autosave yes|no} and {c:--autosave-seconds N}
of the Editor override the settings for one run.
.endif
.h2 Revision, description and author [file-revision]
.ix Revision
.ix Description of a document
.ix Author of a document
.ix Revision number
.ix Description marker
.ix Author marker
.ix Revision marker
Every document has three pieces of information that are kept with its settings
and shown on the panels Save File and New File: the description (a line that
says what the document is), the author, and the revision.  The markers
Description, Author and Revision put them into the text when it is printed.
.table 13 47
Item | Rules
Description | Any text.  A new document has none, unless one was typed on a
 | New panel.
Author | Any text.
Revision | A number with three decimals, such as {c:1.007}.  A new document
 | is {c:1.000}.
.end
.h3 The rule for the revision [file-revision-rule]
.ix Revision, rule
.ix Revision, typed
.ix Save, revision
A Save raises the revision number when the document has changed, and leaves
it when the document has not.
.table 34 26
Situation at Save | Revision written
The Revision field is as it was shown, and | Raised by one thousandth
 the document has been changed since it was | (1.000 gives 1.001).
 last saved, or the Description or the |
 Author was changed on the panel |
The Revision field is as it was shown, and | Unchanged.
 nothing was changed |
The Revision field was typed over with | That number, as typed.
 another number |
{k:Ctrl-S}, document changed | Raised by one.
{k:Ctrl-S}, nothing changed | Unchanged.
Autosave | As {k:Ctrl-S}.
.end
The number in the panel is read as digits, a point and up to three more digits:
{c:2} is 2.000, {c:1.5} is 1.500 and {c:1.0005} is refused with "Not a valid
revision number".  Negative numbers and letters are refused.  The Revision
field of the New
panel is always used for the new document, so that the revision of the
template is not carried over.  If a Save fails, the revision and the changed
flag are put back as
they were.
